{"verdict":"true","basis":"confirmed","reason":"In src/jira.ts:4, `getIssue` calls `fetch(`${JIRA}/issue/${key}`)` with no options: no `signal`, no `AbortSignal.timeout`, and no wrapper that races the request against a timer. Its only caller, the `issue` handler in src/routes.ts:48, awaits the result directly and also has no timeout. Nothing else in the repository sets one: there is no undici dispatcher configuration or server-level request timeout, and package.json has no dependencies. The only limits are Node's built-in undici defaults (about 300 s for headers and 300 s between body chunks). So a Jira server that stalls holds each `/issue` request open for minutes. If Jira keeps sending body data slowly, the request can stay open indefinitely, because the body timer restarts with every chunk. The finding is accurate that the application sets no timeout. It overstates the impact when it says the call hangs forever, because in the plain-stall case Node's defaults cut it off after about 5 minutes. The same gap exists in `searchAssigned` (jira.ts:10) and ci.ts:4.","evidence":[{"location":"src/jira.ts:3","code":"export async function getIssue(key: string): Promise<unknown> {
